<p>At least 22 migrants died after a rubber boat traveling from Tobruk in eastern Libya drifted for six days in the Mediterranean before rescuers found it off Crete, Greek authorities said. A Frontex vessel rescued 26 people. Survivors told the Greek coastguard that bad weather, along with a lack of food and water, led to deaths from exhaustion during the journey. The survivors included 21 Bangladeshis, four South Sudanese, and one Chadian national, according to the Greek coastguard. They also said a smuggler ordered bodies thrown into the sea after passengers died. Greek authorities arrested two men who are under investigation over accusations tied to migrant smuggling and deaths during the voyage. The case underscores the continuing human toll on the central and eastern Mediterranean migration routes, where rescue operations still save lives even as dangerous crossings continue.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Immediate medical response: Greek authorities said rescuers took two survivors to a hospital in Heraklion on Crete after the rescue.</li><li>Survivor-led investigation: Officials pieced together much of the voyage from statements by the people rescued from the boat, showing how often these cases depend on testimony from exhausted survivors.</li><li>Route variation: Greek authorities said the boat left Tobruk bound for Greece, a reminder that departures from Libya do not only feed the Italy-bound central Mediterranean route.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Greek coastguard: Officials said the deaths stemmed from the conditions of the voyage itself — prolonged exposure, no food or water, and bad weather — rather than from a single sudden wreck. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cnv8z1lvn8ro'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Survivors: Their accounts focused on what happened aboard after passengers began dying, alleging that a smuggler kept issuing orders and told others to cast bodies into the sea. (<a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/twenty-two-migrants-die-off-greek-coast-after-six-days-sea-afp-reports-2026-03-28/'>Reuters</a>)</li><li>Greek judicial authorities: Investigators treated the episode as an alleged trafficking case linked to deaths during transport, moving it quickly from a rescue response to criminal proceedings on Crete. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/28/22-people-die-after-six-days-adrift-in-boat-attempting-to-reach-europe-from-libya'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1986412/22-migrants-die-off-greece-after-six-days-at-sea'>22 migrants die off Greece after six days at sea</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/28/22-people-die-after-six-days-adrift-in-boat-attempting-to-reach-europe-from-libya'>Two Sudanese men face court in Greece after at least 22 people die off Crete coast</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cnv8z1lvn8ro'>Twenty-two migrants die off Greek coast after six days at sea</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/03/28/22-migrants-die-off-the-coast-of-crete-after-six-days-at-sea'>22 migrants die off the coast of Crete after six days at sea</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/twenty-two-migrants-die-off-greek-coast-after-six-days-sea-afp-reports-2026-03-28/'>Twenty-two bodies are recovered from a vessel found adrift off the coast of Crete, Greece, according to the Greek coast guard. Twenty-six people are rescued by Frontex. Several survivors said some people were also thrown overboard into the Mediterranean Sea on the orders of a human trafficker during the journey.</a> - reuters.com</li></ul>